\f1\i\fs22 Gerry Hardman
\f2\i0 \

\f1\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>G. Hardman\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jihad Man
\f2\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Ajent Jones scribbled on the cocktail napkin and slapped the pencil down so hard it was a wonder neither it nor the bar top broke. He drained the glass half-empty, slammed it down, and demanded of the barkeep, \'91Another.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bar tender hesitated. \'91Sir, with respect, perhaps you\'92ve consumed a sufficiency?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s just tonic.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender looked nervously at the bin under the counter, brimming with napkins Agent Jones had scribbled on with an officious and altogether intimidated violence the past ninety minutes or so. If anything he was growing angrier. \'91Well, sure,\'92 the bartender conceded, \'91but\'92s zero sugar. That stuff will take you down faster than a bad batch of Bex.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones raised an eyebrow. \'91Is that a drug?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91To be honest, I don\'92t know, sir. But it took my great-grandmother down \'85 ate her kidneys, they say.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You think I\'92m a drug addict? I don\'92t even consume alcohol.\'92\

\f1\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Good thing too
\f2\i0 , the bartender didn\'92t say. 
\f1\i Who knows what this blockhead would be like drunk
\f2\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones glared. The bartender wilted. \'91No ice, right?\'92 he said sunnily as he grabbed a glass and pushed the soda gun into it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ice is an adulterous garnish,\'92 Agent Jones sermonised.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ah, yes. I recall you saying that before.\'92 The bartender arranged a fresh napkin before Agent Jones, and laid the tall glass of tonic upon it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones gestured with four fingers. 
\f1\i More.\

\f2\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender frisbeed a half-inch wad of folded paper across the bar. It stopped spinning near Agent Jones\'92 left bank of knuckles. Agent Jones took a napkin from the top and placed it ready for scribbled. \'91It all connects,\'92 he uttered in exasperation as he scribbled again. In the dim greasy light of the bar, his graphite notes twinkled silver, making them hard to read. But Agent Jones knew what the word read, which is why he stopped writing. If he\'92d followed 
\fs18 GYGES
\fs22  with 
\fs18 DISC
\fs22 , he\'92d have to eat the napkin, or ram it down the bartender\'92s gullet like he was a 
\f1\i foie gras
\f2\i0  duck. One corner of Agent Jones\'92 mouth almost curled into a smile, at the prospect, but he arrested it on the parade ground of his face before any of the other features fell out of place. With jagged lightning strikes of the pencil, he crossed out the word, then wrote 
\fs18 ELOISE
\f1\i\fs22 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tax, I\'92m losing it!
\f2\i0  he swore under his breath, broke the pencil in the three places. With a complete absence of grace, he clutched the napkin and scrunched into a shape resembling a dumpling, and pushed it two knuckles into his tonic. The liquid surged over the top until he retrieved the napkin, which he swallowed in one awkward indelicate gulp.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sir, would you like to see the menu?\'92 the bartender asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Quack, quack,\'92 Agent Jones said, through the pulp in his mouth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender couldn\'92t parse the ill-pronounced words and, fearing Agent Jones was choking, quickly topped his glass up. The last thing he needed was a customer choking to death on his watch. Especially if he\'92d failed to sell the customer any bar snacks when he was clearly hungry or demented. Either way, management would penalise him for the lost sale.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f1\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It all connects.
\f2\i0  Agent Jones knew it. From the beginning, intelligence was strong; Shakti Enterprises was behind the theft of the Disc. Just another move in the chess game of a cold peace between them and PAPER. Whether Shakti was behind the missing Gyges Disc or not, PAPER discovered Travis Teh would be, if not the actual thief, then the final fence. Agent Jones quickly set up a 
\f1\i Blank Contract
\f2\i0  with some poor Cloudborne to retrieve the Disc from Teh. The blank\'97Agent Jones forgot his name\'97considered the prospect of a quick death a kindness, as they often do, in return for generous financial support for his family. The blank dutifully blew Travis Teh away\'97Agent Jones had seen the evidence\'97but had failed to meet for the hand-off of the Disc. Mysteriously, the blank had died (early) on the street the night before. And there was no sign of the Disc.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It grew only more complicated from there; enter Sarah Jacobson and Sukrit Supaporn. Agent Jones was sure Jacobson was the one mentioned in that cabinet meeting, the Holy Momma whipping up religious fervour among the Elvi, who subsequently broke Supaporn out of PAPER\'92s Truth facility. With tanks supplied by Shakti Enterprises. Then, to resolve the siege at the mansion, Shakti\'97Eloise Shakti herself!\'97disabled all unfriendly weapons\'97police and PAPER\'97and whisked the Holy Momma, with two of your collaborators, to safety.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones was sure Eloise Shakti acquired the Gyges Disc during the siege, if she didn\'92t possess it already. What kinds of unspeakable weapons might Shakti produce with Gyges technology? And would she sell them to PAPER? Or keep them for the Holy Momma\'92s holy crusade? A dangerous game, Eloise was on borrowed time, now she betrayed her customers. All of them would have painted a target on her by now. Her only escape was to corner the arms race with new technology, and the Gyges Disc may well be the foundation of it. If she could mollify just a single customer, all the others would fall into line or risk being wiped out by a better-equipped adversary.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Unless \'85 unless, this was all part of an escalation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A new fear gripped Agent Jones. If war broke out, the newly religious Elvi fanatics might fight with their greatest asset: music\'97a weapon Agent Jones, and indeed all of Ulyssia, had no counter for. If so, then hostilities had broken out already; Ulyssia was succumbing to a craze of Elvi-mania, of earwig melodies and enigmatic lyrics, reportedly written by someone they called the Great Teacher. Could that be Sukrit Supaporn? Credible reports had Supaporn now living in a large, recently built recording complex in the Ghetto. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tax in heaven! The Elvi could conquer the world; pacifying the masses with music that had both sex appeal and emotional authenticity. It would be a simple step from there to subduing the populace with dodgy diets and drugs. Oh so many artery-hardening, life-shortening kilojoules! And drugs; don\'92t lose sight of the drugs.\

\f1\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>We don\'92t stand a chance
\f2\i0 , Agent Jones realised, 
\f1\i unless we stop it now.
\f2\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones imagined it all in glittering, blinding rhinestones and bacon-flavoured horror: a holy jihad launched across the world by the Holy Momma, aided by the Great Teacher, and equipped by the greatest arms dealer in the world. Like those old sci-fi movies that Agent Jones never saw. But, instead of fierce Zen Sufi sand-warriors cutting the hearts out of their foes and taking their water, an army of Elvi would shake-rattle-and-roll their way to victory using the power of \'85 entertainment. Agent Jones almost gagged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Making it worse, Elvi men were persuasively handsome, and the women were, well, handsome too (many wore sideburns like the men). They were still very attractive, and it gave Agent Jones the 
\f1\i ick
\f2\i0  something rotten.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The more Agent Jones thought about, the more he hoped the Elvi did what the Elvi always did: resort to violence. Guns, tanks, pool cues, and broken toilet bowls were easier to fight than cultural memes and chart-topping tunes.\

\f1\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It all connects.
\f2\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>What of Jihad Man? Call it intuition. Agent Jones felt in his paranoid bones, Jihad Man was part of this somehow. Which is unsurprising: all the psychometric instruments PAPER\'92s inhuman resources department effectively selected for candidates suffering from apophenia, the human tendency to see imaginary patterns. Which also explains why many of them are religious nuts, seeing P&L charts in the clouds which predicted the future, and so on. Agent Jones performed so well on these tests, his scores had slipped straight off the bell curve and into some other statistical reality entirely. Which made Agent Jones a darling of the upper echelons of PAPER the political elite who interacted with it. When something messy needing doing, just aim Agent Jones and sit back while he derived all kinds of interconnections in the service of justifying his mission, which he rarely failed to accomplish.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But Agent Jones was failing to accomplish this mission, and so he resorted to searching for ever more elaborate patterns, involving an ever increasing cast of bad actors. A kind of Grand Unified Theory of Evil, you might say, though Agent Jones would never think of it in these terms. To him, Evil was a singular amorphous toxic cloud in which particulates were all connected, if only in an amorphous, cloudy way. You need to see the cloud 
\f1\i and
\f2\i0  all the little clouds inside it, he was fond of telling himself.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Which is all to say, whether or not there was any factual relationship between Gerry Hardman and Eloise Shakti: given enough time, pencils and paper napkins, Agent Jones was hard-wired to see one.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Of course the obvious link was Alexander Koalemos, Eloise\'92s police officer son, but Agent Jones scarcely gave it consideration. Everyone knew Alexander Koalemos was an idiot. And Eloise always kept her business at so many arms\'92 lengths (always wise for an arms dealer) from her son, they could span the continent. In recent years, briefs on Eloise profiled her increasing concern over a succession plan. Much to the relief of security organisations everywhere, Alexander would not be inheriting the family farm. Or the family nuclear silos.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On impulse, Agent Jones grabbed another napkin and wrote:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f1\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jihad of the Elvi
\f2\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He was pretty sure that was a safe thing to write. Anyone reading it would just think him crazy, which most people thought anyway. There\'92d be no need to moisten and swallow the napkin. He contemplated the title, then wrote underneath: \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f1\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Elvian Jihad\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f2\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kind of like a Butlerian Jihad, except with entertainers instead of butlers. Then his eyes went wide and he wrote:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f1\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Elvian Jihad Man
\f2\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones\'92 blood cooled. He felt an all-over frisson tingle down his neck and flow all the way to his nethers, a sense of the jigsaw falling into place, which a therapist should have counselled him years ago could be his apophenia assuming control of his lizard brain. 
\f1\i Thar be dragons!
\f2\i0  the therapist\'97any therapist\'97should have warned, dragons being nothing more than a taxing big lizard with a tiny lizard brain.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry Hardman was the lizard in the room; Agent Jones was sure of it. He didn\'92t trust Hardman from the moment he turned the war cabinet into a personal audience with the CEO. Agent Jones still had know knowledge of what they discussed; not even he had security clearance for the bugs in the most secure office in the land. How had such a recently promoted Police Commissioner exerted such influence over the CEO of Ulyssia.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Suspicious.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>So was the previous Commissioner\'92s retirement, who had only recently been appointed when the CEO gained power. Retirements weren\'92t, of themselves, suspicious. But the burning of effigies on the lawn of the Commissioner\'92s church, dressed as white supremacists\'97and which forensics found were stuffed with dog-digested human remains\'97starkly marked the transition of the previous Commissioner from 
\f1\i presumed on vacation
\f2\i0  to 
\f1\i presume retired
\f2\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There was also the matter of the CEO firing the Director of Freedom within days of the war cabinet, and not yet appointing a replacement. The Department of Freedom had direct oversight of national security and, therefore, PAPER. Agent Jones was unable to learn anything about who might be directing PAPER from behind the curtain. For the time being, PAPER seemed neither Proactive nor Preemptive.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And Gerry Hardman had all the scent of a double agent. Agent Jones could smell double agents, even under the Layer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And when Agent Jones read-in the fresh Commissioner Harding on relevant high-profile national security issues, he was surprised when no busy-body probing questions came. Not a single question out of order.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Probably because Jihad Man knew the answers to all of them already.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>None of this was coincidence, Agent Jones concluded, because 
\f1\i there were no coincidences
\f2\i0 . The only coincidence he might credit? \'97when someone said something smart-mouthed and then he knocked their teeth out. Now, 
\f1\i that
\f2\i0  was a coincidence.\

